{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:6ad946653892cf84c035f95faba07799134436e61f255e64983e600e1606a9ad","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.18,"B":0.43,"C":0.27,"D":0.12}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Every cell re-derived from MY OWN posterior CQ3 and CQ2 under the lexical-priority lock, first match wins. The lock makes CQ6 a deterministic function of those two answers, so an independently assigned cell would be an inconsistency rather than a forecast. MY CQ3: P10 0.9 / P50 1.9 / P90 2.9, sigma_right 0.78, sigma_left 0.80.\n\nSTEP 1 — A if coupled growth >= 2.6%. z = (2.6 - 1.9)/0.78 = 0.897, P = 0.185. A = 18.\nI REJECT THE \"A MUST SIT BELOW THE STANDALONE 16.2%\" RULE, and this is where the red-teamer landed his cleanest conceptual hit on the whole panel. The Scout asserted that \"any panelist placing A at or above 16.2% has failed to couple\" and the Bayesian inherited it at A = 11. That inverts the arithmetic. CQ6-A's lexical gate is a GROWTH threshold on a PPP aggregate that is 34% weighted to China, not a TFP-breakout threshold. On measured China at 4.2% and OECD-38 at 1.8%, A clears: 0.66 x 1.8 + 0.34 x 4.2 = 1.19 + 1.43 = 2.62% — a normal cyclical expansion plus the measured utilities and construction value added of the build, with NO transformative TFP event required. The locked AI-Q1 and AI-Q6-Breakout marginals are OECD-relevant frontier-capability questions; A is a headline-growth question with a third of its mass in a smoothed administrative series. On the GROWTH gate, coupling makes A EASIER than the standalone breakout, not harder. A = 18, read directly off my own band, not assigned.\nFLAG, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: A's LABEL (\"debt/GDP stabilizes via growth, broad wage gains\") is narrower than A's GATE. My own CQ4 P50 of 2.2% real against G7 g ~1.2% puts r - g at +0.9 to +1.1 pp, so inside much of my 18 points debt/GDP keeps rising even while the coupled PPP aggregate clears 2.6%. I follow the lock (rule 1 is a pure growth gate) and report the tension.\n\nSTEP 2 — else C if OECD-38 core >= 2.7% AND growth < 2.0%. P(growth < 2.0) = P(z < 0.128) = 0.551. Inflation leg derived from MY CQ2 conjunct A of 0.76 at the >= 2.50% gate over 2027-36: raising the gate to 2.70% and narrowing to 2032-36 pulls down for mean reversion (Türkiye disinflating further into the 2030s, G7 converging) and up for fiscal dominance, giving 0.44. Panelist spread on this leg was 0.265-0.55 and it is ENTIRELY derivative of the conjunct-A disagreement — since I am at 0.76 on A, I am obliged to the upper range, and 0.44 is the coherent value. Positive dependence, because my own weakest-link story is SUPPLY-side and therefore stagflationary (administered care prices, HV interconnection and transformer rationing at >160-week lead times, five-year turbines, copper near record): P(core >= 2.7 | growth < 2.0) = 0.44 x 1.12 = 0.49. C = 0.551 x 0.49 = 0.270 -> 27.\n\nSTEP 3 — else D if growth < 1.7% AND core < 2.0%. P(growth < 1.7) = P(z < -0.25 on sigma_left) = 0.401. P(core < 2.0 over 2032-36) unconditionally ~0.21 under the PPP-composition arithmetic — this is the structural fact that STARVES D, since sub-2.00% on that aggregate needs the G7 block near 1.5% AND Türkiye in low single digits AND Mexico near 3% simultaneously. Conditional lift to 0.30 (x1.45) because a sub-1.7% world is disproportionately the post-capex-bust, BIS-Box-C labour-income-deficit state, and because the supply-squeezed slice of the low-growth region has already been claimed by C. I DO NOT follow the red-teamer to 0.18: he pushes the composition argument past where it can carry, since D needs sub-2.0% only over a five-year SUBWINDOW in a world where a capex bust has already landed. D = 0.401 x 0.30 = 0.120 -> 12.\n\nSTEP 4 — B = residual = 100 - 18 - 27 - 12 = 43. SUM = 100 EXACTLY.\n\nEXPLICIT CONSISTENCY CHECKS DEMANDED BY THE LOCK.\n(i) AGAINST MY OWN CQ3: P50 1.9% sits inside B's 1.7-2.5% band, so B must carry the plurality and A cannot. 43 > 27 > 18 > 12 satisfies the lock's instruction that a P50 of 2.1% forbids most mass on A — it binds harder at 1.9%.\n(ii) A = 18 IS P(CQ3 >= 2.6) computed off my own stated P90 of 2.9 and sigma_right of 0.78. Not independently assigned, and no shading applied, since my rejection of the 16.2% floor removes the only reason to shade.\n(iii) AGAINST MY OWN CQ2: C's inflation leg (0.44 unconditional at the 2.70% gate on the 2032-36 subwindow, 0.49 conditional) is strictly below my CQ2 conjunct-A marginal of 0.76 at the lower 2.50% gate over the longer window, as a higher threshold on a subwindow must be. C = 27 < 100 x 0.49, so C cannot exceed what my own CQ2 permits.\n(iv) D requires BOTH legs. My CQ2 implies 1 - 0.76 = 0.24 as the upper bound on sub-2.50% core over 2027-36; my 0.21 at the tighter <2.00% gate sits inside it. D is correctly the SMALLER of the two low-growth cells despite my carrying an AI-Q4 impairment prior of ~0.73, and the reason is purely the inflation-measure composition — which I name in coupling_tension as the load-bearing non-AI assumption in my whole payload.\n(v) B absorbs the lock's two leftover cells. Implied leftover = P(growth < 2.0) - C - D = 0.551 - 0.270 - 0.120 = 0.161, inside B; the remaining ~0.27 of B is the 2.0-2.6% growth band plus the growth-2.0-2.6%-with-core->=2.7% cell that rule 2 does not claim. B at 43 falling BELOW the standalone Jagged Diffusion mass of 47.2% is permitted here (unlike in the lower-A payloads, where it had to exceed it) because my A is larger: coupling moves mass into A's growth gate via measured China rather than out of it.\n(vi) COHERENCE WITH CQ4: B's \"steep real term premia\" is literally my CQ4 decomposition (+0.40 pp of real term-premium widening against a net-zero Delta r*), which is why B is modal; and C's \"financial repression or term-premium spikes\" is my CQ4 P90 branch at 3.4%, consistent with C at 27 rather than 15.\n(vii) COHERENCE WITH CQ5: C + D = 39 points of elevated-rate-vol / correlation-stress mass, which bounds CQ5 at ~0.31 as shown in cq5_reasoning; CQ5 = 26 sits inside.\n\nSIMULATION: disclosed as PARTIAL — only the growth leg is modellable and the model cannot place mass between C and D because it has no price level. Its growth readings would put A at or above 50% on five of nine scenarios. Rejected as a point input for the four structural reasons in cq3_reasoning.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.970Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the load-bearing plank of my entire payload. CQ2 falls from 44 to ~26 (conjunct A from 0.76 to ~0.48). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 27 to ~16, D from 12 to ~22, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix, probability mass shifts from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional row (-6.5 in C) toward my best (+6.0 in D), and cuts gold's weighted contribution hard. This is the cheapest single observation that falsifies me, and it is a measurement question, not a macro one.\",\"signpost\":\"The OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% y/y for four consecutive quarters, with Türkiye's core in single digits and Mexico's below 3.5% — i.e. the five EM members do NOT hold the aggregate above the G7 core.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Refutes the MOVE level-scaling argument EMPIRICALLY rather than theoretically, which is the whole of my move above the Wave-1 centre. CQ5 falls from 26 to ~14 (conjunct A from 0.50 back to ~0.30). CQ4 P90 falls from 3.4 to ~3.0 on a smaller term-premium tail. CQ6-C falls ~3 points to B. Gold's C-column number falls ~1.5 pp and long nominal sovereigns improve ~1.0 pp in every column.\",\"signpost\":\"The ICE BofA MOVE Index three-year average over 2027-2029, taken from ICE's own index data rather than an aggregator, prints below 90 WHILE the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yield averages above 3.75% over the same three years.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"IMF WEO October 2029 edition\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the measured-versus-true-China channel that is worth ~0.3 pp of my CQ3 P50 and ~5 points of my CQ6-A, and is the main place I moved toward the red-team. CQ3 P50 falls from 1.9 to ~1.7 and P90 from 2.9 to ~2.7; CQ6-A falls from 18 to ~12 with the mass going to B (+5) and D (+1). China equities/onshore duration: the equity half is damaged in every column and the duration half improves. It would also partly vindicate the Bayesian seat's narrower P90 of 2.65 against mine.\",\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO prints combined 2030 and 2031 China real GDP growth below 3.5%/yr on the REALIZED/ESTIMATE line (not the forecast line) — i.e. the official series stops printing near its target.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies BOTH places I departed upward from the Wave-1 CQ4 centre: the non-US-convergence delta term (+0.20 pp) and my cut of the Reinhart-Sbrancia repression wedge from -0.20 to -0.14. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.2 to ~1.4 and P90 from 3.4 to ~2.9; P10 falls from 1.15 to ~0.6, restoring the repression tail that the 3:1 TIPS-anchor likelihood ratio currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 26 to ~17 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. CQ6-D rises from 12 to ~19. G7 long nominals, TIPS and cash are all mis-priced too low in my 2026-2030 buildout column.\",\"signpost\":\"The G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (10y nominal minus 10y inflation swap) averages below 1.00% for twelve consecutive months, with the 10y JGB real yield still below 0.50%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the physical-energization cap that holds my CQ3 P90 at 2.9 and that makes power/grid (+7.5) and construction/trades (+6.5) my two best 2026-2030 rows. CQ3 P90 rises to ~3.2 and P50 to ~2.0; CQ6-A rises from 18 to ~24 with B falling. My upward correction to AI-Q4 (toward 0.73) reverses toward the locked 0.66, and realized AI-Q3 load returns toward the locked 998 TWh P50. In the matrix, power/grid buildout falls from 7.5 to ~4.5 and construction from 6.5 to ~4.0 as the scarcity rent evaporates, while the AI-complex column rises ~3 pp in A and B.\",\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks AND gas turbine lead times below 36 months, on OEM order-book disclosures or NERC/EIA reporting — against >160 weeks and five years respectively as of Q1 2026.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'growth'."}}